[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2608291500-store-write-amplification-is-a-vector-shaped-problem"
  :adr/title "Store write amplification is a vector-shaped problem: measured waste and a staged design"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-29"
  :adr/related "adr-2608291300-bots-return-to-murakumo-main-and-the-pool-stops-rotating"
  :adr/body "## Why now\n\nOwner instruction 2026-08-29: 「無駄がないように最適化を設計してね」, following the journal-compaction design review the same day. This ADR is the design. Status is `proposed`: nothing here is implemented, and each phase below carries its own acceptance gate so a later session can land phases independently and honestly.\n\n## What was measured, 2026-08-29, live resident store\n\nAll numbers from this machine's store during active resident work (92-bot workforce on murakumo-main, load1 30-75).\n\n**Write rate and cadence.** 13.2 journal entries/min, p50 18.8 KB, mean 20.7 KB. The 4 MiB byte trigger fires first, at ~202 entries ≈ every 15 minutes, and every fold rewrites the whole snapshot. Snapshot is 6.9 MB, so during activity the store writes ~28 MB/h of snapshot plus ~16 MB/h of journal to persist far less than that of actual change.\n\n**Where the journal bytes go.** In one 852 KB post-compaction window:\n\n| op family | ops | bytes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `[:bots :runs <id> :messages]` whole-vector assoc | 6 | 454,702 |\n| `[:bots :goal-jobs <id> :job/events]` whole-vector assoc | 10 | 353,136 |\n| everything else | ~150 | ~44,000 |\n\n**95% of journal bytes are whole-vector rewrites of vectors that only grew at the end.** `state-delta` already descends maps with `identical?` pruning (scalar updates like `:turn-count` journal ~80 bytes), but replaces vectors whole -- deliberately, because whole-value `:assoc` is what makes crash-replay idempotent (its own docstring says so). A resident run accumulates `:messages`; each model call therefore journals the entire transcript again: O(n²) journal bytes over a run's life. The measured 158-tool-call run is the worst case this shape produces.\n\n**Where the snapshot bytes sit.** 6.9 MB total: `:goal-jobs` 2.75 MB (223 jobs, most terminal, awaiting gc retention), `:runs` 1.77 MB, `:turn-history` 377 KB, `:contexts` 365 KB, `:conversations` 284 KB, bot registry 245 KB, `:chronicle` 530 KB. **~65% of every 15-minute snapshot rewrite is terminal or historical data that has not changed.**\n\n## Invariants that must survive any change\n\nThese are the load-bearing properties the current design bought with production incidents; a phase that breaks one is wrong even if faster:\n\n1. **Crash-idempotent replay** -- a crash between snapshot write and journal truncation leaves records already folded; re-applying them must change nothing.\n2. **Bound checked BEFORE append** (the 2026-08-27 deadlock: journal froze 218 bytes under its cap for 8.5 h).\n3. **Orphan-journal detection** -- a journal is replayed only against the snapshot it declares.\n4. **Journal life bounded to one process** (fold at startup).\n5. **A record larger than the journal budget snapshots instead** (measured 393 KB mail-sync transaction).\n\n## Design\n\n### Phase 0 -- snapshot identity by digest, not size (correctness, ~10 lines)\n\n`journal-belongs-to-snapshot?` compares byte COUNTS. Two different snapshots of coincidentally equal size would accept a stale journal and replay it silently. Record `:base-sha256` alongside `:base-bytes` (kept for old-journal tolerance one release), compare digest when present. Acceptance: a doctored journal with matching bytes but wrong digest is archived, not replayed.\n\n### Phase 1 -- an idempotent append op for grow-only vectors (~95% of journal bytes)\n\nAdd one op type: `{:op :append :path p :from n :value [items]}`.\n\n- **Emission** (`state-delta`): when both sides are vectors, `(<= (count a) (count b))`, and `(= a (subvec b 0 (count a)))` (cheap under structural sharing -- shared prefixes win `identical?` per element), emit `:append` with `:from (count a)`; otherwise whole `:assoc` exactly as today. No caller changes: runs, job events, turn history, traces all benefit or fall back transparently.\n- **Application** (`apply-op`), and this is where invariant 1 lives: let `len` be the current vector's length. `len = from` -> append. `len >= from + (count items)` -> **no-op** (this record was already folded; the crash-window case). Anything else -> throw `:store/invalid-journal` -- the journal does not belong to this state and pretending otherwise corrupts it.\n- **Compatibility**: an older build reading a new journal throws `Unknown state journal operation` at startup -- loud, which is the designed failure. Rollback procedure: fold (start+stop the new build, or delete after confirming fold) before downgrading. Same posture the schema check already takes.\n- **Expected effect** (from the table above): journal volume during resident work drops ~95% -> the 4 MiB trigger stretches from ~15 min toward hours; snapshot rewrite frequency falls proportionally. Combined disk writes during activity: ~44 MB/h -> roughly 8-10 MB/h.\n- **Acceptance gate**: (a) measured before/after journal bytes/hour on this store, published in this ADR; (b) a test that folds mid-stream and re-applies the same `:append` record, asserting the no-op branch by its own reason; (c) a test where `len < from` throws `:store/invalid-journal` by name; (d) the existing replay suite unchanged and green.\n\n### Phase 2 -- the snapshot stops carrying its own graveyard (~65% of fold cost)\n\nTerminal `goal-jobs`, finished `runs`, and turn-history beyond the SLO's 24 h window move to an append-only archive (`data/state-archive.edn`, one record per line, written with the same `append-durable!`, rotated by gc at a size bound, never rewritten). The hot snapshot keeps active work plus the 24 h `bot-slo` window. Two birds: fold cost falls with snapshot size (6.9 MB -> ~2 MB), and the 2026-08-29 journal-review complaint -- history that evaporates at compaction, making 'no turns' and 'not yet visible' the same observation -- gets the CLAUDE.md-sanctioned answer, an append-only event series, instead of readers grepping a WAL.\n- **Order note**: implement AFTER Phase 1 and remeasure first -- if Phase 1 alone stretches folds to hourly, Phase 2's payoff shrinks and its reader-surface changes (`bot-slo` reading hot + archive) may not be worth it yet.\n- **Acceptance gate**: `bot-slo/evaluate` answers identically over a store before and after the split (same windows, same numbers), plus measured fold cost before/after.\n\n### Phase 3 -- trigger economics (config-level, do last)\n\n`journal-max-entries` 256 exists because 'many tiny records make replay slow' -- but with Phase 1 the tiny records ARE the journal, and 256 scalar ops fold a 6.9 MB snapshot to persist a few KB. Raise the entry cap (1024) and consider making the byte trigger proportional (`min(16 MiB, snapshot-bytes)`) so the amplification ratio per fold is bounded below ~2x regardless of snapshot size. Only after Phases 1-2 land and the new steady state is measured -- tuning triggers first would just be moving the same waste around.\n\n## What this deliberately does not do\n\n- No new storage engine, no D1/SQLite/kotobase migration for the app-local store -- the WAL+snapshot shape is fine; the waste is in op granularity and snapshot diet.\n- No journal-as-history: observability wants the Phase 2 archive, not a longer-lived WAL.\n- No compression: the dominant cost is rewriting unchanged bytes, not their encoding.\n\n## 現在地\n\nMeasured and designed 2026-08-29. **Phases 0 and 1 are implemented the same day**: `:base-sha256` snapshot identity (digest of the in-memory string at write time, never a re-read; old digestless journals still replay on matching bytes) and the `:append` op with its length-and-content guard. Suites: store-journal 17/36, host-bounds 5/21 on BOTH the JVM and nbb, store+agreement+work-partition+drive-migration+bots 157/725 -- all green; negative controls: a blind `into` fails `replaying-a-record-twice` and the guard test by name, and dropping the digest comparison fails `equal-length-is-not-identity` by name. Phase 1's acceptance gate (a) is now MEASURED, in three points on the live resident under comparable activity. (1) BEFORE (build f7e24e4): 13.2 entries/min at mean 20.7 KB, 3.1 MB per 13.4-minute window (~14 MB/h), 95% whole-vector rewrites. (2) Append op alone (build e59bae4): :messages whole-rewrites 455 KB -> 0, but :job/events still 2.7 MB whole -- its 200-cap ledger trimmed on EVERY append, so at the cap its prefix moved each transact and :append could never apply. That finding produced the same-day follow-up (1f0ff12): eviction hysteresis, run to 220 and trim back to 200, so 19 of 20 appends are pure tails. (3) AFTER (build 1f0ff12): 36.0 entries/min -- MORE activity than the before-window -- at mean 1.4 KB (p50 0.7 KB), ~3.0 MB/h; whole-vector :job/events rewrites 0, hot-path appends 88 ops totalling 48.7 KB. Per-entry cost fell 93%; at ~3 MB/h the 4 MiB fold trigger fires every ~80 minutes instead of ~15, cutting snapshot rewrites from ~28 MB/h to ~5 MB/h. Phase 2 was then implemented the same day on owner instruction. The cold archive is `state-archive.edn` beside the snapshot (append-only, fsynced, rotated aside at 64 MiB, never rewritten; `store/archive-append!`), and gc's sweep moves three things through it: terminal goal-jobs (full value archived at compaction -- compact-days 2 -> 1 -- or at an uncompacted drop), saved `[:bots :runs]` checkpoints whose goal-job is terminal or gone (measured 1.77 MB / 25% of the snapshot as finished transcripts; runs holding :pending-call approvals, non-Goal runs, and any run with a resumable job are kept), and per-bot turn-history beyond 8 days outside the newest 50 (the SLO asks 24 h and 7 d questions; a quiet bot keeps its history regardless of age). The archive write is INSIDE the sweep's transaction, before the drops commit: a throw aborts the whole sweep and the hot copies stay -- losing a sweep is a delay, dropping unarchived history is a deletion. Acceptance: `the-slo-answers-identically-across-the-cold-split` holds `bot-slo/evaluate` byte-equal across the split with a positive control that the split moved things; `a-sweep-that-cannot-archive-drops-nothing` pins the fail-closed contract, and its negative control (a swallowed archive failure) fails it 4 ways by name. gc-test 14/85, combined affected suites 190/842, lint at the 83-error baseline. Fold-cost before/after on the live store is appended below after deployment. Phase 3 stays parked until the post-Phase-2 steady state is measured.\n\nPre-existing and NOT this change: `bin/test-portable-cljs` fails on unmodified main `895795f` with `Could not find namespace: kotoba.kir` -- the same-day store-core kotoba migration broke the portable suite's 'depends on nothing outside src and test' property. host-bounds-test was run on nbb directly instead (green). Left for the migration's author or a follow-up."}]
